      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Plasma levels of remnant-like particles (RLP) is one of the predictive markers for coronary artery disease (CAD), and the inhibition by RLP of endothelium-dependent vasorelaxation has been reported. We attempted to clarify whether or not RLP, which inhibits endothelium-dependent vasorelaxation, affects nitric oxide (NO) production and NO synthase (eNOS) levels in cultured endothelial cells."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "RLP were obtained from postmortem blood of subjects who died of CAD. Modification by RLP of acetylcholine-induced relaxation of rabbit aorta, and changes in NO production and (eNOS) in cultured bovine endothelial cells were examined."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "RLP at 750 and 1500 microg triglyceride/ml inhibited vasorelaxation, and at 5-160 microg triglyceride/ml, concentration-dependently inhibited NO production. However, (eNOS) did not decrease after incubation with RLP."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Postmortem RLP from subjects who died of CAD do not change the amount of (eNOS), but rather, inhibits its activity and attenuates endothelium-dependent vasorelaxation."
        }
      ]},
